5.8, "sigma_total": 6.61, "baseline_margin": 4.9, "analyst_adjustment": 0.0, "key_factors": ["Favorable 2026 Democratic midterm environment in a dead-even district", "Rematch dynamics: Luria has name ID and matching money, both raising about $7.3M", "Eight of ten expert ratings call a Democratic flip; Kalshi and Polymarket price Luria at 85-88 percent", "Kiggans's incumbency and thin recent polling keep uncertainty elevated"], "rationale": "A strong Democratic environment, Luria's rematch name recognition and even fundraising, a credible mid-October CNU poll showing her up three, and a rating consensus of Lean-to-Likely D all point to a modest Democratic edge, while Kiggans's incumbency and two Tossup ratings keep this short of Likely D.", "watch": "New polling or a late shift in the national environment toward Republicans would move this back toward a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Jen Kiggans (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Virginia's 2nd District is a rematch between Republican incumbent Jen Kiggans and Democrat Elaine Luria, the former one-term incumbent Kiggans unseated in 2022. The seat sits on even partisan terrain, and the 2026 midterm environment favors Democrats. The only recent public poll, a Christopher Newport University survey of likely voters in mid-October, shows Luria ahead 46-43, while a July poll from a Democratic firm had the race tied. Both candidates have raised more than $7 million, leaving neither with a meaningful financial edge.\n\nEight of ten professional raters call the race a Democratic flip — four Likely D and four Lean D — while Cook and Inside Elections rate it a Tossup. Kalshi and Polymarket price Luria's win chances at 85 to 88 percent. The forecast lands on a modest Democratic edge, with Kiggans's incumbency and the district's swing history keeping the outcome in doubt.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7963, "margin": 4.98}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8168, "margin": 5.5}, {"date